tory the party that controls  the White House and that's the Democrats  right now almost always lose seats in  the midterms that's been a stronger  Trend in the house as you can see here  over the decades than the Senate but  both sides of Congress tend to get a  shellacking which is what President  Barack Obama described the massive  losses he suffered in the 2010 midterms  and of course there are a lot of factors  that determine the outcome of the  midterms they're of course major events  and policies of the party in power but  also voter turnout which side can get  more of their people to the polls and  structural advantages this year Republic  Republicans in the house just have a  better chance of flipping seats and have  fewer of their own to defend the  situation in the Senate is a bit more of  a fair fight but still a challenge for  Democrats and then the unknowns how will  newly redrawn voting maps for House  Seats impact the party's chances what  about new voting regulations in some  places due to baseless claims of voter  fraud and of course Donald Trump he  isn't on the ballot but several of his  preferred candidates are and how they  and Republicans more broadly perform  could signal things to come for 2024 and  American democracy Beyond  all right straight to Washington DC now  where we can speak to our correspondent  Stefan Simons Stefan it's a big election  day today the polls have opened but  people have actually been sending in  their votes for a couple of weeks  already haven't they can you tell us how  this could potentially affect the  outcome  yeah hi they have indeed in record  numbers actually and how could this  impact the results and when we get  results that is the really really  interesting question here and why is  that because Florida for example they're  already processing or the right term for  this is canvassing send in votes bail in  votes  um but States like Pennsylvania and  Wisconsin the State lost there forbid  this to happen before Election Day  meaning they start canvassing processing  those mail-in ballots just today they  started about an hour ago to do this and  there are hundreds and thousands of this  what that means is that results from  Florida we will may have tonight later  today or early tomorrow morning  Pennsylvania Wisconsin and a few other  states where they just start to count  those votes  um well that could take days until we  have really an accurate picture of who  won what race for a gubernatorial races  Governor or senate or congress all right  so it might take a couple of days let's  take a look at the both the houses of  Congress which are currently controlled  by the Democrats but Republicans really  only need a few extra seats to take  control don't they so tell us how easy  it will be for them to do that  well in the house is pretty much a done  deal they need to pick up five seats to  really uh kick the Democrats out of the  majority in the House of Representatives  that should be absolutely possible for  the Republicans they are absolutely  hopeful and positive and excited about  that perspective of course send it a  little bit of a different thing now  it is just taking one one  seat in the senate for the Republicans  to negate the majority the Democrats  right now have and that's only because  of the deciding vote of Vice President  Kamala Harris  but it is really not that easy for  Republicans at least it is not  considered that easy to really topple  this or move a majority in the Senate  house yes everybody expects a a  significant loss for the Democrats in  the Senate maybe maybe and the Democrats  are very hopeful they can hold on for  the Senate why is this important because  real laws will be decided before they go  to the president for the  for his signature and to become lost  they have to go to the Senate it's not  that the House of Representatives can  make a decision create a law and then it  goes to the Senate and their rubber  stamp is if you have the majority in one  in the house but not in the Senate it's  getting really difficult all right  Stefan Simon's in Washington D.C thanks  so much for that  now the world is watching the U.S  midterms as the outcome could of course  change U.S foreign policy now while  issues like the economy have been  dominating the campaign the war in  Ukraine is also costing U.S consumers in  many ways and some are now questioning  how much longer the U.S should support  Ukraine's fight against Russia  Russia's war against Ukraine is one the  U.S has not been able to look away from  Americans have already sent more than 60  billion dollars worth of assistance into  this war zone Democrat President Joe  Biden has promised to continue to do all  it takes to defend Ukraine sovereignty  As Americans head to the polls and these  midterm elections U.S Congress is  expected to turn more Republican and the  big question everybody is asking is what  impact that will have on U.S assistance  to Ukraine and other allies well the  answer depends on who you ask there will  be enough support no matter who controls  which house and given President Biden  still in the white house so I I am quite  confident that very very strong support  will continue for Ukraine yet one  prominent Republican started sending a  very different signal ahead of the  elections Kevin McCarthy is the top  Republican in the House of  Representatives he says he believes  Americans would no longer be willing to  write a blank check to Ukraine in the  face of a biting recession back home  after all the soaring prices of gas food  and housing topped the list of worries  for ordinary Americans Democrats fear  that a republican majority in Congress  could soon make Ukraine a domestic  bargaining chip but Paul so more than  two-thirds of Americans back U.S  assistance to Ukraine even if there is a  republican majority in the house and or  the Senate  I think perhaps the experience of  working together so closely  and so conf